{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfukfxCzEEN2oAqeuKPbj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, with gradual deployments. Instead of `wrangler deploy` (which switches 100% of traffic to the new version immediately), use `wrangler versions upload` and then `wrangler versions deploy` to split traffic between versions, e.g. 90% old and 10% new, and ramp up over time.\n\nOne caveat: a Durable Object runs a single instance globally, so it can't run old and new code simultaneously. Gradual deployments for DOs mean the Worker code in front is split across versions, but the DO itself is updated to the new version for all instances when you deploy the new version at any percentage. Also, Durable Object migrations (class renames, deletions, new classes) can't be used with gradual deployments; those require a full `wrangler deploy`.\n\nIf you need old DO code to keep running for a while, the usual approach is to make your changes backward compatible, or to ship the new logic as a separate DO class or namespace and migrate traffic to it yourself.\n\nI'm working from memory of Cloudflare's docs here, so check the current gradual deployments page for any recent changes."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2262,"cache_read_input_tokens":535,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2262},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9c90df24-f897-4698-9853-040adeabc627","uuid":"029ba5a1-832d-4291-a63a-92a94388cb9e","timestamp":"2026-10-11T03:39:05.148Z","request_id":"req_011CfukfwzbM6bZfyXDzPQfc"}
